# id|name|family|milk|origin|rind|texture|signature|make|nuance|source
abbaye-tamie|Abbaye de Tamié|washed|Cow|Savoie, France|Washed|Supple, creamy|A monastic washed-rind wheel from Tamié Abbey in Savoie|Surface care develops a savoury rind around a yielding paste.|Resemblance to Reblochon does not make the two names interchangeable.|https://www.cheese.com/tamie/
abondance|Abondance|alpine|Cow|Haute-Savoie, France|Natural, cared-for rind|Firm and supple|A Haute-Savoie mountain cheese with fruity and hazelnut-like notes|Raw cow milk becomes a pressed mountain wheel; maturation develops its nutty character.|Compare region and wheel identity, not just the shared word nutty.|https://www.cheese.com/abondance/
appenzeller|Appenzeller|alpine|Cow|Appenzell, Switzerland|Washed|Firm, sometimes small-eyed|A Swiss mountain wheel associated with an herbal brine wash|Rind washing and maturation contribute a spicy, savoury character.|Herbal washing is a better study clue than claiming every wheel tastes identical.|https://www.cheese.com/appenzeller/
asiago|Asiago|alpine|Cow|Veneto and Trentino, Italy|Natural|Supple young; firmer and crumbly aged|An Italian mountain cheese with distinct fresh and aged forms|Pressato is the younger, softer form; d’allevo develops a firmer paste through longer maturation.|Always specify the age or form before predicting texture.|https://www.cheese.com/asiago/
azeitao|Azeitão|softsheep|Sheep|Arrábida area, Portugal|Natural, yellowish|Soft and buttery|A small Portuguese sheep cheese set with cardoon near Arrábida|Plant-derived coagulant helps create a rich, yielding paste and a subtly bitter herbal character.|Do not confuse this small Portuguese cheese with the Spanish tortas.|https://www.cheese.com/azeitao/
banon|Banon|goat|Goat|Provence, France|Chestnut-leaf wrapped|Soft, creamy|A Provençal goat cheese wrapped in chestnut leaves|The leaf wrapper is a distinguishing presentation and maturation feature.|A leaf wrapper alone does not identify milk species: other wrapped cheeses use cow or sheep milk.|https://www.produits-laitiers-aop.fr/produits/banon/
beaufort|Beaufort|alpine|Cow|Savoie, France|Natural, cared-for rind|Firm, smooth|A Savoie mountain wheel known for a concave heel|Cooked, pressed curd produces a firm paste with buttery, fruity and nutty notes.|Its curved wheel edge is more specific than the broad label Alpine.|https://www.fromage-beaufort.com/
bergkase|Bergkäse|alpine|Cow, typically|German-speaking Alpine regions|Varies by producer|Firm to hard|A German term meaning mountain cheese rather than one universal recipe|Many examples are cooked, pressed cow-milk wheels with savoury, nutty maturation.|The full regional name and producer are needed to establish a particular specification.|https://www.cheese.com/bavarian-bergkase/
bleu-auvergne|Bleu d’Auvergne|blue|Cow|Auvergne, France|Natural|Creamy, moist|A cow-milk blue associated with Auvergne, not a sheep-milk blue|Blue development creates a savoury, mushroom-like character through the paste.|Cow milk distinguishes it from Roquefort; it does not distinguish it from every other French blue.|https://www.fromage-aop-bleu-auvergne.com/en/bleu-auvergne-pdo/cheese/identity-card/
bleu-causses|Bleu des Causses|blue|Cow|Causses, southern France|Natural|Creamy and yielding|A Causses blue that uses cow milk rather than Roquefort’s sheep milk|Cave maturation contributes a pronounced blue character and melting texture.|Shared geography and blue veins do not establish identical milk or identity.|https://dairy-products-from-france.com/cheeses/bleu-des-causses/
brick|Brick|washed|Cow|Wisconsin, USA|Washed in traditional aged examples|Semi-soft|A Wisconsin original traditionally pressed with actual bricks|Young versions can be mild; traditional aged Brick develops a stronger washed-rind character.|Specify mild or aged Brick instead of assuming all examples have the same intensity.|https://www.widmerscheese.com/product/foil-pack-aged-brick-14-ounces/
brie-meaux|Brie de Meaux|bloomy|Cow|Brie region, France|Bloomy white|Softening from the rind inward|A large, flat raw-milk Brie wheel from the Meaux tradition|Surface ripening develops a creamy layer and mushroom-like character.|The protected full name is more specific than the broad word Brie.|https://www.cheese.com/brie-de-meaux/
brillat-savarin|Brillat-Savarin|bloomy|Cow, cream-enriched|France|Bloomy in ripened examples|Rich, dense and creamy|A cream-enriched French cheese named for a gastronome|Added cream gives the cheese a particularly rich, buttery character.|Cream enrichment is a stronger discriminator than assuming every white rind is Brie.|https://www.cheese.com/brillat-savarin/
brin-amour|Brin d’Amour|bloomy|Sheep|Corsica, France|Herb-coated|Soft to supple|A Corsican sheep cheese with an aromatic herb coating|Exterior herbs add a recognisable herbal aroma to the sheep-milk paste.|Herbs are a coating clue, not evidence of blue veins or an ash layer.|https://www.cheese.com/brin-damour/
burrata|Burrata|fresh|Cow, commonly|Puglia, Italy; also made elsewhere|Rindless|Elastic pouch, flowing filling|A stretched-curd pouch enclosing stracciatella and cream|The outer shell holds strands of curd mixed with cream; cutting it reveals two textures.|It is not simply a solid ball of mozzarella.|https://www.cheese.com/burrata/
cabrales|Cabrales|blue|Cow; may include sheep and goat|Asturias, Spain|Natural|Creamy to crumbly|An Asturian cave-ripened blue whose milk can be cow alone or a blend|Cave conditions support extensive blue development and a pronounced flavour.|Do not memorize a compulsory three-milk blend; the actual milk composition can vary.|https://www.cabralesturismo.es/elaboracion-del-queso-de-cabrales
caciocavallo|Caciocavallo|fresh|Cow, typically|Southern Italy|Natural|Elastic young, firmer aged|A pear-shaped stretched-curd cheese traditionally hung in pairs|Pasta filata involves working curd in hot water; ageing firms the paste.|The pasta-filata family includes both fresh and aged cheeses. This one is often aged.|https://www.cheese.com/caciocavallo/
caerphilly|Caerphilly|territorial|Cow|Welsh origin; also made elsewhere|Natural in traditional examples|Moist, crumbly centre|A Welsh-origin territorial known for a fresh acidic, crumbly centre|Traditional maturation can create a softer layer beneath the rind while the centre stays crumbly.|Generic Caerphilly and Traditional Welsh Caerphilly are not equivalent naming claims.|https://www.cheese.com/caerphilly/
camembert-normandie|Camembert de Normandie|bloomy|Cow|Normandy, France|Bloomy white|Soft and yielding when ripe|A small Norman raw-milk bloomy wheel, distinct from a large Brie|Surface ripening changes the texture from the outside toward the centre.|The source list’s “di Normandie” spelling is corrected to “de Normandie.” Generic Camembert is broader.|https://www.cheese.com/camembert-de-normandie/
cantal|Cantal|territorial|Cow|Auvergne, France|Natural|Firm, increasingly crumbly with age|A large Auvergne cylinder whose curd is milled before final pressing|Curd handling creates a close paste that develops more forceful flavour with age.|Jeune, entre-deux and vieux denote different maturation stages, not different milk animals.|https://www.cheese.com/cantal/
cashel-blue|Cashel Blue|blue|Cow|County Tipperary, Ireland|Natural|Creamy|An Irish cow-milk blue made by the Grubb family|Its creamy paste and blue development give a rounded, tangy character.|Irish origin and maker are stronger identity clues than blue veining alone.|https://www.cheese.com/cashel-blue/
castelmagno|Castelmagno|blue|Predominantly cow; some sheep or goat permitted|Piedmont, Italy|Natural|Crumbly, becoming more complex with age|A Piedmont cheese that can develop blue veining with maturation|Its friable paste differs from the flowing texture of many young blue cheeses.|Visible blue is variable; do not require dramatic veining in every young example.|https://www.cheese.com/castelmagno/
chabichou|Chabichou du Poitou|goat|Goat|Poitou, France|Fine, wrinkled surface|Chalky young, softening under rind|A small Poitou goat cheese shaped like a truncated cone or bung|Lactic character and surface ripening produce a tangy paste that changes with age.|Shape and origin distinguish it from a log or a flattened ash-coated disc.|https://www.cheese.com/chabichou-du-poitou/
chaource|Chaource|bloomy|Cow|Champagne and Burgundy area, France|Bloomy white|Chalky centre, creamier edge|A tall French cow-milk cylinder with a lactic centre beneath a white rind|Surface ripening softens the outer paste while a young centre can remain chalky.|A white rind does not imply the cream enrichment of Brillat-Savarin.|https://www.cheese.com/chaource/
cheddar|Cheddar|territorial|Cow, typically|English origin; made worldwide|Clothbound, natural or rindless|Firm; more brittle with age|A style associated with stacking and turning curd slabs before milling|Cheddaring changes the curd’s structure before salting and pressing; maturation deepens flavour.|Cheddar alone is not confined to four English counties. Specific protected names have narrower rules.|https://www.cheese.com/cheddar/
cheshire|Cheshire|territorial|Cow|Cheshire tradition, England|Natural or clothbound in traditional examples|Moist and crumbly|An English territorial known for a bright, acidic, crumbly bite|A relatively friable paste separates the style from the denser body of many Cheddars.|White and coloured versions exist; colour alone is not a reliable identification.|https://www.nealsyarddairy.co.uk/blogs/cheese/6-iconic-british-styles-of-cheese-at-neal-s-yard-dairy
classic-blue-log|Classic Blue Log|goat|Goat|Massachusetts, USA|Blue-mould surface|Soft, dense centre|A Westfield Farm goat log with blue mould on its exterior|Surface blue development contrasts with the internal veining of most familiar blues.|A blue exterior does not automatically mean a blue-veined interior.|https://www.cheese.com/classic-blue-log/
colby|Colby|territorial|Cow|Wisconsin, USA|Usually rindless|Moist and springy|A Wisconsin washed-curd style often coloured orange|Washing and cooling the curd helps produce a milder, moister body than many mature Cheddars.|Orange colour cannot distinguish Colby from coloured Cheddar.|https://www.cheese.com/colby/
comte|Comté|alpine|Cow|Jura massif, France|Natural, cared-for rind|Firm, supple to granular with age|A French Jura wheel associated with Montbéliarde and French Simmental cattle|Cooked, pressed curd and long maturation develop varied fruity, toasted and savoury notes.|Its green and brown bands describe grading, not simply age. Taste varies by wheel and season.|https://www.comte.com/questions-de-gout/
cougar-gold|Cougar Gold|territorial|Cow|Washington State, USA|Canned presentation|Firm and crumbly when mature|A Washington State University cheese sold in a distinctive can|Maturation develops a sharp, nutty profile in a white Cheddar-style cheese.|Canned presentation is not the same as a processed melting cheese product.|https://creamery.wsu.edu/
coulommiers|Coulommiers|bloomy|Cow|Brie area, France|Bloomy white|Soft, creamy when ripe|A Brie-family round generally smaller and thicker than Brie de Meaux|Surface ripening gives a creamy, buttery paste and mushroom-like rind character.|Size is a useful clue, but the actual label is needed to establish the named cheese.|https://www.cheese.com/coulommiers/
crottin|Crottin de Chavignol|goat|Goat|Chavignol and Sancerre area, France|Natural, developing surface mould|Soft young; firm and dry aged|A small goat cheese shaped as a compact drum rather than a long log|Drying and ripening can make the cheese dense and much more concentrated.|The word crottin alone does not establish the Chavignol origin.|https://www.crottindechavignol.fr/up/documents/depliant-aop-chavignol-en.pdf
edam|Edam|alpine|Cow|Dutch origin; made elsewhere|Often waxed|Smooth and firm|A Dutch-origin cheese commonly presented as a red-waxed ball|The paste is mild and nutty when young and becomes firmer with maturation.|Wax is packaging, not an edible microbial rind; not every Edam has identical coating.|https://www.cheese.com/edam/
emmentaler|Emmentaler|alpine|Cow|Swiss origin; regional versions elsewhere|Natural|Firm, elastic with large eyes|A Swiss-origin cooked-curd wheel famous for large rounded eyes|Gas produced during ripening forms the characteristic openings in the paste.|Large eyes are not the defining feature of every Swiss cheese; distinguish Le Gruyère AOP.|https://www.cheese.com/emmental/
epoisses|Époisses|washed|Cow|Burgundy, France|Washed, orange-toned|Soft to spoonable|A Burgundian washed-rind round associated with Marc de Bourgogne|Repeated surface washing develops its aromatic rind and yielding paste.|Rind aroma can be stronger than the flavour of the paste; assess both separately.|https://www.cheese.com/epoisses-de-bourgogne/
feta|Feta|fresh|Sheep; may include goat|Greece|Rindless, brined|Crumbly and moist|A Greek brined white cheese based on sheep milk, with goat milk permitted|Brining contributes a salty, tangy profile; the paste breaks rather than stretching.|Protected Greek Feta is not a universal name for every white brined cow-milk cheese.|https://www.cheese.com/feta/
fiore-sardo|Fiore Sardo|hard|Sheep|Sardinia, Italy|Natural, often smoke-influenced|Firm, increasingly granular|A Sardinian sheep cheese associated with traditional smoking|Raw sheep milk and maturation produce a robust paste with a possible smoky note.|Fiore Sardo and Pecorino Sardo are separate protected names, not synonyms.|https://www.cheese.com/fiore-sardo/
fontina|Fontina Val d’Aosta|alpine|Cow|Aosta Valley, Italy|Cared-for brownish rind|Supple, elastic|An Aosta Valley mountain cheese with a supple melting paste|Whole cow milk produces a buttery, nutty cheese with useful melting properties.|Distinguish the Aosta cheese from generic cheeses sold as fontina elsewhere.|https://www.cheese.com/fontina/
fourme-ambert|Fourme d’Ambert|blue|Cow|Auvergne, France|Natural|Supple, creamy|A tall cylindrical Auvergne blue often described as relatively gentle|Internal blue development gives earthy flavour in a creamy, close-textured paste.|Its tall format helps distinguish it from a broader Bleu d’Auvergne wheel.|https://www.fourme-ambert.com/
gaperon|Gaperon|bloomy|Cow, in the example studied|Auvergne, France|Bloomy white|Soft, yielding|An Auvergne dome flavoured with garlic and cracked pepper|Added garlic and pepper contribute flavour beyond that created by ripening alone.|Aromatics in the paste distinguish it from an unflavoured bloomy-rind cheese.|https://www.mons-fromages.com/en/gaperon-fermier-194.html
garrotxa|Garrotxa|goat|Goat|Catalonia, Spain|Grey natural mould|Semi-firm|A Catalan goat wheel with a velvety grey rind|Maturation creates a mild, milky paste with earthy and nutty notes.|Grey mould on the surface is not automatically ash or internal blue veining.|https://www.cheese.com/garrotxa/
gloucester|Gloucester / Double Gloucester|territorial|Cow|Gloucestershire tradition, England|Natural or clothbound in traditional examples|Firm and buttery|An English territorial whose Double form is typically rich and orange|Annatto commonly colours Double Gloucester; its paste is usually smoother than a crumbly Cheshire.|Single Gloucester and Double Gloucester are distinct names, not simply two wheel sizes.|https://www.cheese.com/double-gloucester/
gorgonzola|Gorgonzola|blue|Cow|Northern Italy|Natural|Creamier dolce; firmer piccante|An Italian cow-milk blue with dolce and piccante expressions|Maturation and make choices produce contrasting creamy or firmer, more assertive versions.|Do not assign one texture or intensity to every Gorgonzola.|https://www.cheese.com/gorgonzola/
gouda|Gouda|alpine|Cow, commonly; other milks exist|Dutch origin; made worldwide|Waxed, coated or natural|Supple young; hard and crystalline aged|A Dutch-origin style associated with washing curds to reduce lactose|Removing lactose-containing whey limits substrate for further acid development.|Washed curd describes a vat operation; it does not mean the rind was washed.|https://www.cheese.com/gouda/
grana-padano|Grana Padano|hard|Cow|Po Valley area, Italy|Hard natural rind|Hard and granular|An Italian grana with a nine-month minimum maturation|Partly skimmed cow milk becomes a cooked, pressed cheese. The specification permits lysozyme except in Trentingrana.|Permitted does not mean present in every wheel; read the product label.|https://www.granapadano.it/public/file/SpecificationsGBOct2022-50256.pdf
gruyere|Le Gruyère AOP|alpine|Cow|Western Switzerland|Cared-for natural rind|Firm and dense|A raw-milk Swiss mountain cheese without Emmentaler’s characteristic large eyes|Cooked, pressed curd develops a savoury, nutty paste that melts well.|This lesson specifies Swiss Le Gruyère AOP; similarly named products elsewhere can differ.|https://www.gruyere.com/en/le-gruyere-aop/characteristics
halloumi|Halloumi|fresh|Sheep and goat; cow may be included|Cyprus|Rindless, brined|Springy, holds shape when grilled|A Cypriot brined cheese whose curd is cooked in hot whey|Heating the curd contributes to a firm, resilient texture suited to grilling.|Holding shape on a grill does not make it the same process as acid-set Paneer.|https://www.cheese.com/halloumi/
havarti|Havarti|alpine|Cow|Danish origin|Usually rindless|Supple with small irregular openings|A Danish-origin cow cheese with small openings and a buttery body|The paste is usually mild and elastic when young, sharpening as it matures.|Its small openings differ from the large rounded eyes associated with Emmentaler.|https://www.cheese.com/havarti/
humboldt-fog|Humboldt Fog|goat|Goat|California, USA|Bloomy, with ash|Chalky centre, creamy edge|A Cypress Grove goat wheel with a central ribbon of vegetable ash|Surface ripening develops a cream line beneath the rind around a fresher centre.|The dark ribbon is ash, not blue mould; Morbier also has a dark line but uses cow milk.|https://www.cypressgrovecheese.com/usa/en/cheese/soft-ripened-cheeses/humboldt-fog
jack|Jack / Vella Dry Jack|territorial|Cow|California, USA|Rindless young; rubbed rind on Dry Jack|Supple young; hard aged|A California family ranging from mild Monterey Jack to hard Dry Jack|Dry Jack matures much longer than young Jack and can become a grating cheese.|A question about Jack must specify the variant before assuming age or texture.|https://www.cheese.com/monterey-jack/
idiazabal|Idiazábal|hard|Sheep|Basque Country and Navarre, Spain|Natural; smoked versions exist|Firm and compact|A northern Spanish sheep cheese associated with Latxa and Carranzana breeds|Pressed curd and maturation yield a nutty, savoury paste; some versions are smoked.|Smoke is optional rather than a reliable requirement for every wheel.|https://www.cheese.com/idiazabal/
jarlsberg|Jarlsberg|alpine|Cow|Norwegian origin; licensed production elsewhere|Often coated|Supple with rounded eyes|A Norwegian-origin eye-forming cheese with a sweet, nutty character|Ripening creates conspicuous eyes in the pale, elastic paste.|Swiss-style does not mean Swiss origin; check the producer for actual manufacturing country.|https://www.cheese.com/jarlsberg/
kasseri|Kasseri|fresh|Sheep; may include goat|Greece|Thin or minimal|Firm, elastic|A Greek sheep-based stretched-curd cheese suited to melting|Working acidified curd in hot water creates a cohesive pasta-filata structure.|Unlike crumbly Feta, its defining process is stretching, not simply storing in brine.|https://www.cheese.com/kasseri/
laguiole|Laguiole|territorial|Cow|Aubrac, France|Natural|Firm and supple|An Aubrac cow cheese made as a large pressed cylinder|Maturation develops an aromatic, savoury paste in the mountain cheesemaking tradition.|Fresh tomme used for aligot and a mature named cheese are not the same stage of production.|https://www.cheese.com/laguiole/
lancashire|Lancashire|territorial|Cow|Lancashire, England|Clothbound or natural in traditional examples|Buttery or crumbly, depending on form|An English territorial with creamy, tasty and crumbly expressions|Traditional Lancashire can combine curds from successive days, developing a buttery texture.|The word Lancashire alone does not guarantee a single crumbly texture.|https://www.nealsyarddairy.co.uk/blogs/cheese/6-iconic-british-styles-of-cheese-at-neal-s-yard-dairy
langres|Langres|washed|Cow|Langres plateau, France|Washed, orange-toned|Softening around a firmer centre|A washed-rind cylinder with a sunken top called a fontaine|Surface maturation softens the paste; the top depression is a useful presentation clue.|Adding wine or spirit to the depression is a serving tradition, not proof of identity or a required ingredient.|https://www.cheese.com/langres/
la-serena|La Serena|softsheep|Sheep|La Serena, Extremadura, Spain|Natural|Creamy, sometimes spoonable|A Merino-milk Spanish cheese set with thistle in La Serena|Plant coagulant and ripening help develop a rich paste with a characteristic bitter edge.|It resembles Torta del Casar but belongs to a different named production area.|https://www.cheese.com/la-serena/
limburger|Limburger|washed|Cow|Historical Limburg region; also made elsewhere|Washed|Semi-soft, creamy when ripe|A pungent washed-rind style originating in historical Limburg|Surface bacteria develop an aromatic orange-brown rind around a milder paste.|Country of origin and current manufacturing country are different facts.|https://www.cheese.com/limburger/
livarot|Livarot|washed|Cow|Normandy, France|Washed, encircled with strips|Soft and yielding|A Norman washed-rind round traditionally bound with five reed strips|The strips support the cheese and explain its Colonel nickname; the rind develops a strong aroma.|Strip-bound Livarot is a different visual cue from square Pont-l’Évêque.|https://www.produits-laitiers-aop.fr/produits/livarot/
mahon|Mahón-Menorca|hard|Cow|Menorca, Spain|Natural, often rubbed|Supple young; hard aged|A Menorcan cow cheese commonly formed with rounded square corners|Cloth shaping and maturation produce a distinctive format and increasingly savoury paste.|Spanish origin alone does not imply sheep milk, as Manchego uses.|https://www.cheese.com/mahon/
manchego|Manchego|hard|Sheep|La Mancha, Spain|Patterned natural rind|Firm, firmer and granular with age|A La Mancha cheese made from Manchega sheep milk|Pressed curd develops a nutty, savoury paste; the mould echoes traditional woven patterns.|A basket pattern is not proof of Manchego; other cheeses use patterned moulds too.|https://www.cheese.com/manchego/
manouri|Manouri|fresh|Sheep or goat whey, enriched with milk or cream|Greece|Rindless|Soft, dense and creamy|A Greek whey cheese enriched for a rich, gentle flavour|Milk or cream added to whey produces a mild, creamy cheese suited to sweet or savoury service.|Unlike Feta, the key clue is enriched whey rather than a sharp brined curd.|https://www.cheese.com/manouri/
maroilles|Maroilles|washed|Cow|Northern France|Washed, orange-red|Supple, creamy|A square northern French washed-rind cheese|Regular rind care develops a pronounced aroma around a soft paste.|Square shape alone cannot separate it from Taleggio; include country and regional identity.|https://www.produits-laitiers-aop.fr/produits/maroilles/
mascarpone|Mascarpone|fresh|Cow cream|Lombardy, Italy|Rindless|Smooth and thick|An Italian fresh product made by acidifying heated cream|Cream, rather than whey, supplies the rich body and mild buttery flavour.|Do not treat Mascarpone and Ricotta as the same ingredient simply because both can go in desserts.|https://www.cheese.com/mascarpone/
maytag-blue|Maytag Blue|blue|Cow|Iowa, USA|Natural; sold foil-wrapped|Moist and crumbly|An Iowa blue made by Maytag Dairy Farms from local cow milk|Cave maturation develops blue flavour in a tangy, crumbly paste.|Its foil is different from the pear-spirit-soaked leaf wrap of Rogue River Blue.|https://maytagdairyfarms.com/pages/cheese-care
mimolette|Mimolette|hard|Cow|Northern France|Rough natural rind|Firm to very hard|An orange spherical cheese with a rough, pitted natural rind|Annatto colours the paste; traditional rind maturation involves cheese mites.|An orange paste plus a wax coating suggests a different presentation from its rough natural rind.|https://www.cheese.com/mimolette/
montasio|Montasio|alpine|Cow|Friuli Venezia Giulia and Veneto, Italy|Natural|Supple young; hard aged|A northeastern Italian cow cheese sold in multiple maturation stages|Ageing turns a relatively mild paste into a firmer, more intense cheese.|Compare the stated maturity rather than expecting every wheel to be a hard grating cheese.|https://www.cheese.com/montasio/
mont-dor|Mont d’Or / Vacherin du Haut-Doubs|washed|Cow|French Jura|Washed, spruce-banded|Soft, spoonable when ripe|A seasonal French Jura cheese encircled in spruce and boxed|Spruce supports the very soft cheese and contributes to its aromatic context.|This lesson covers the French cheese; Swiss Vacherin Mont-d’Or has a separate specification.|https://www.mont-dor.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/CDC_MontdOr_19nov2025.pdf
monte-enebro|Monte Enebro|goat|Goat|Ávila, Spain|Blue-grey mould surface|Dense, softening beneath rind|A Spanish goat log with blue mould on the rind rather than veins through the centre|Surface Penicillium roqueforti contributes flavour around a tangy pale paste.|A blue rind is not the same feature as a central ash ribbon.|https://culturecheesemag.com/cheese-library/monte-enebro
morbier|Morbier|alpine|Cow|Jura, France|Washed or rubbed|Supple|A Jura cow cheese with a horizontal black vegetable-carbon line|The line preserves a traditional visual identity within an uncooked pressed cheese.|The line is not blue mould; distinguish the cow-milk paste from Humboldt Fog’s goat milk.|https://www.cheese.com/morbier/
munster|Munster|washed|Cow|Vosges and Alsace, France|Washed|Soft and yielding|An aromatic French Vosges washed-rind round|Rind care produces a pungent surface and a supple, creamy paste.|French Munster is distinct from mild American Muenster with an orange-coloured edge.|https://www.cheese.com/munster/
neufchatel|Neufchâtel|bloomy|Cow|Normandy, France|Bloomy white|Soft, sometimes slightly grainy|A Norman bloomy cheese often shaped as a heart|Surface maturation develops a savoury white rind around a soft paste.|French Neufchâtel is not the same product as American reduced-fat cream-cheese-style Neufchatel.|https://www.cheese.com/neufchatel/
ossau-iraty|Ossau-Iraty|hard|Sheep|Béarn and French Basque Country|Natural|Firm and supple|A French Pyrenean sheep-milk tomme from Béarn and the Basque Country|Pressing and maturation create a nutty paste with a natural rind varying from yellow-orange to grey.|Both it and Manchego use sheep milk; regional identity separates them.|https://www.ossau-iraty.fr/decouvrir-l-ossau-iraty
paneer|Paneer|fresh|Cow or buffalo|South Asia|Rindless|Firm, cohesive|A fresh heat-and-acid-set cheese that holds its shape in cooking|Acid added to hot milk forms curds that are drained and pressed.|It differs from Halloumi’s rennet-set, whey-cooked process despite a shared ability to hold shape.|https://www.cheese.com/paneer/
parmigiano|Parmigiano Reggiano|hard|Cow|Defined area of northern Italy|Hard natural rind|Hard and granular|An Italian grana requiring at least twelve months of maturation|Milk, salt and rennet are the core ingredients; the consortium does not allow additives or preservatives.|Grana Padano has a different minimum age and specification; granular texture alone cannot separate them.|https://www.parmigianoreggiano.com/en-US/faq
paski-sir|Paški sir|hard|Sheep|Pag Island, Croatia|Natural|Firm, increasingly flaky|A Croatian sheep cheese from the island of Pag|Maturation concentrates its savoury character and produces a firmer, granular paste.|The island origin separates it from similarly firm Spanish or Italian sheep cheeses.|https://www.cheese.com/paski-sir/
pecorino-toscano|Pecorino Toscano|hard|Sheep|Tuscany and permitted neighbouring areas, Italy|Natural|Supple young; firm aged|A Tuscan sheep cheese offered in fresh and aged forms|Pecorino refers to sheep milk; ageing changes the strength and firmness of this particular cheese.|Toscano is not Romano or Fiore Sardo. The regional name matters.|https://www.pecorinotoscanodop.it/en/pecorino-toscano-pdo/the-sheep-and-the-milk/
piave|Piave|alpine|Cow|Belluno, Veneto, Italy|Natural|Firm, becoming granular|A Belluno cow cheese with Fresco, Mezzano and Vecchio forms|Cooked curd and maturation produce progressively firmer, more savoury cheeses.|Its Italian mountain origin is distinct from the Po Valley grana identities.|https://www.formaggiopiave.it/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Technical-Specification_PIAVE.pdf
pleasant-ridge|Pleasant Ridge Reserve|alpine|Cow|Wisconsin, USA|Washed or rubbed|Firm and supple|An Uplands Cheese wheel made during the May–October pasture season|Pasture-season raw milk and Alpine-inspired methods yield a rich, savoury cheese.|The making season is not a rule restricting the months in which aged cheese can be sold.|https://uplandscheese.com/our-cheese/
pont-eveque|Pont-l’Évêque|washed|Cow|Normandy, France|Washed, pale to reddish|Supple and melting|A Norman washed-rind cheese with a square or rectangular outline|Surface maturation develops a buttery, aromatic paste with a relatively gentle flavour.|Its angular format distinguishes it from the strip-bound round of Livarot.|https://www.produits-laitiers-aop.fr/produits/pont-leveque/?action=pdf_fiche_technique
raclette|Raclette|alpine|Cow, typically|Swiss and French Alpine traditions|Natural or washed, depending on example|Supple, readily melting|A cheese and dish associated with melting a surface and scraping it|The name relates to scraping; the melted cheese is often served with potatoes and accompaniments.|Generic Raclette is broader than a named protected regional version.|https://www.cheese.com/raclette/
reblochon|Reblochon|washed|Cow|Savoie, France|Fine washed rind with light surface bloom|Soft and supple|A small Savoyard raw-milk round associated with tartiflette|Gentle surface care develops a creamy paste and nutty character.|Its small format and softer paste contrast with the much larger firm Beaufort wheel.|https://www.cheese.com/reblochon/
red-hawk|Red Hawk|washed|Cow, cream-enriched|California, USA|Washed, orange-toned|Very creamy|A Cowgirl Creamery triple-cream with a washed rind|Added cream enriches the paste while brine washing develops a savoury surface.|Triple-cream does not necessarily mean a white bloomy rind.|https://www.cheese.com/red-hawk/
red-leicester|Red Leicester|territorial|Cow|Leicestershire tradition, England|Natural or clothbound in traditional examples|Firm and crumbly|An English territorial with annatto-coloured paste and a crumbly body|Maturation develops a nutty, slightly sweet character under the traditional rind.|Its orange colour overlaps with Double Gloucester and Colby; use origin and texture too.|https://www.cheese.com/red-leicester/
ricotta|Ricotta|fresh|Whey or milk from various dairy species|Italian tradition; made worldwide|Rindless|Fine, moist curds|A recooked whey tradition yielding delicate fresh curds|Heating whey recovers remaining proteins; modern milk-based versions also exist.|Check whether the example is whey-based or milk-based rather than assuming all Ricotta is identical.|https://www.cheese.com/ricotta/
robiola|Robiola|bloomy|Cow, goat, sheep or blends, depending on type|Piedmont and Lombardy, Italy|Fresh rindless or surface-ripened|Soft|A northern Italian family name covering multiple milks and rind types|Some examples are fresh and lactic; others ripen under a delicate surface rind.|Ask for the full name and maker. Robiola alone cannot establish one milk blend or protected identity.|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robiola
rogue-river|Rogue River Blue|blue|Cow|Oregon, USA|Grape-leaf wrapped|Creamy and crumbly|An Oregon blue wrapped in grape leaves soaked in pear spirits|Leaf wrapping adds a distinctive presentation and aromatic context to the mature blue cheese.|The 2019 World Cheese Awards champion is a specific cheese, not a generic name for any leaf-wrapped blue.|https://www.cheese.com/rogue-river-blue/
roncal|Roncal|hard|Sheep|Roncal Valley, Navarre, Spain|Natural|Firm and compact|A raw sheep-milk cheese from Navarre’s Roncal Valley|Pressing and maturation create a firm, savoury paste.|It shares sheep milk with Manchego but not the La Mancha production identity.|https://quesoroncal.com/es/home/
roquefort|Roquefort|blue|Sheep|Roquefort-sur-Soulzon tradition, France|Natural exterior|Moist, crumbly|A French sheep-milk blue matured in the Roquefort caves|Blue mould develops through the paste; the cheese has a pronounced salty, tangy character.|Sheep milk separates it from cow-milk Bleu des Causses and Stilton.|https://www.cheese.com/roquefort/
sainte-maure|Sainte-Maure de Touraine|goat|Goat|Touraine, France|Ash-coated surface|Lactic, firmer with age|A long goat log with a straw running through its centre|Surface maturation develops a grey exterior around a tangy paste.|This lesson uses the full Touraine identity; generic Sainte-Maure is less specific.|https://www.cheese.com/sainte-maure-de-touraine/
saint-nectaire|Saint-Nectaire|washed|Cow|Auvergne, France|Natural surface flora, washed during care|Supple|An Auvergne pressed cheese with an earthy rind and yielding paste|Uncooked pressed curd and rind care produce a creamy, hazelnut-like character.|It is a more supple wheel than the large, firmer Cantal cylinder.|https://www.cheese.com/saint-nectaire/
salers|Salers|territorial|Cow|Auvergne, France|Natural|Firm and crumbly|A farmhouse Auvergne cheese tied to the pasture season|Raw milk from grazing cows becomes a large pressed cylinder with complex savoury flavour.|Salers cheese is not automatically made only from Salers-breed cows; breed and cheese name are separate claims.|https://www.cheese.com/salers/
salva-cremasco|Salva Cremasco|washed|Cow|Lombardy, Italy|Washed or brushed|Compact, friable centre; softer edge|A tall square-sided Lombard cheese with a relatively friable centre|Maturation progresses inward, softening the paste just beneath the rind.|Its taller block and firmer centre help distinguish it from flatter Taleggio.|https://www.salvacremasco.com/index.php/il-prodotto/il-prodotto-2
sao-jorge|São Jorge|hard|Cow|São Jorge, Azores, Portugal|Natural|Firm, sometimes open|A firm cow-milk cheese from a Portuguese Atlantic island|Maturation develops a tangy, spicy paste.|Portuguese cheese does not always mean a soft sheep-milk cheese set with thistle.|https://www.cheese.com/sao-jorge/
selles-cher|Selles-sur-Cher|goat|Goat|Loire area, France|Ash-coated|Fine, soft paste|An ash-coated goat cheese shaped as a flattened disc with bevelled sides|Vegetable charcoal colours the exterior; lactic and surface development shape the flavour.|The disc distinguishes it from Valençay’s truncated pyramid and Touraine’s long log.|https://www.aop-sellessurcher.com/le-produit/
serra-estrela|Serra da Estrela|softsheep|Sheep|Serra da Estrela, Portugal|Natural|Buttery young; firmer aged|A Portuguese mountain sheep cheese coagulated with thistle|Plant coagulant and ripening yield a rich paste, with soft and more mature expressions.|Compare mountain origin with Azeitão’s Arrábida area; both use sheep milk and plant coagulant.|https://www.cheese.com/serra-da-estrela/
stilton|Blue Stilton|blue|Cow|Derbyshire, Leicestershire and Nottinghamshire, England|Natural crust|Creamy yet crumbly|An English blue made with pasteurized cow milk in three specified counties|An unpressed cylinder develops a natural crust and blue veins.|This lesson covers Blue Stilton; White Stilton is a different expression.|https://www.cheese.com/stilton/
stinking-bishop|Stinking Bishop|washed|Cow|Gloucestershire, England|Washed in perry|Soft and creamy|An English washed-rind cheese named for a pear used in perry|Perry washing contributes to the strong rind aroma; the interior can taste gentler.|Perry is fermented pear juice, distinct from the grape spirit used in Époisses care.|https://www.cheese.com/stinking-bishop/
taleggio|Taleggio|washed|Cow|Northern Italy|Washed, pink-orange|Soft and supple|A relatively flat square northern Italian washed-rind cheese|Rind care develops a savoury aroma around a yielding paste.|Do not equate pungent rind aroma with equally forceful flavour in the centre.|https://www.cheese.com/taleggio/
torta-casar|Torta del Casar|softsheep|Sheep|Casar de Cáceres area, Extremadura, Spain|Natural|Soft and spoonable|A Spanish thistle-set sheep cheese associated with Casar de Cáceres|Plant coagulant and maturation develop a rich, spreadable paste with a slightly bitter edge.|La Serena is similar in style but has a different protected geographic identity.|https://www.cheese.com/torta-del-casar/
valdeon|Valdeón|blue|Cow; goat may be included|Valdeón Valley, León, Spain|Leaf-wrapped presentation|Creamy to crumbly|A León blue traditionally presented in a leaf wrapper|Internal blue development creates an assertive flavour within a soft paste.|Do not assume every Spanish blue is Cabrales or requires a three-milk blend.|https://www.queseriaspicosdeeuropa.com/
valencay|Valençay|goat|Goat|Berry, France|Ash-coated surface|Soft young, denser aged|A goat cheese shaped as a truncated pyramid with a grey ash coat|Surface ripening changes the texture around the lactic centre.|The Napoleon story is treated as a legend, not a verified production fact.|https://www.cheese.com/valencay/
velveeta|Velveeta|fresh|Dairy ingredients; formulation-specific|USA|Rindless|Smooth, uniform melt|A branded processed dairy product formulated for a smooth melt|Emulsifying ingredients help the product melt evenly instead of separating like some natural cheeses.|Read the current label: it is a formulated cheese product, not a naturally matured wheel.|https://www.kraftheinz.com/velveeta/products/00021000616893-original-cheese
wensleydale|Wensleydale|territorial|Cow, in modern common examples|Yorkshire tradition, England|Varies by producer|Moist and crumbly|A Yorkshire-origin territorial with a fresh, milky, crumbly character|The plain cheese offers a lactic counterpoint to firmer, longer-aged territorials.|Fruit additions are optional flavourings, not what defines the plain cheese.|https://www.cheese.com/wensleydale/
winnimere|Winnimere|washed|Cow|Vermont, USA|Washed, spruce-banded|Soft and spoonable|A winter-made Jasper Hill raw-milk cheese wrapped in spruce bark|A cultured brine wash develops savoury, custardy and woodsy notes.|Compare Harbison’s bloomy rind rather than grouping all bark-wrapped cheeses together.|https://www.jasperhillfarm.com/winnimere/
zamorano|Zamorano|hard|Sheep|Zamora, Spain|Patterned natural rind|Firm and compact|A Zamora sheep cheese associated with Churra and Castellana breeds|Pressing and maturation develop a savoury, nutty paste.|Its patterned exterior can resemble Manchego; the breeds and region distinguish the identities.|https://www.quesozamorano.com/
harbison|Harbison|bloomy|Cow|Vermont, USA|Bloomy, spruce-banded|Soft and spoonable|A Jasper Hill bloomy-rind cheese supported by spruce bark|Surface ripening produces a creamy interior with woodsy and mushroom-like notes.|Bonus from the Winnimere note: the key contrast is bloomy Harbison versus washed-rind Winnimere.|https://www.jasperhillfarm.com/harbison/
